                    text: """
                        static int z_@@NAME@@_has(const z_@@NAME@@_t* _this, @@KEY_T@@ _key);
                        static int z_@@NAME@@_has(const z_@@NAME@@_t* _this, @@KEY_T@@ _key) {
                            return z_@@NAME@@_find(_this, _key, NULL) >= 0;
                        }
                        """
                )
                (
                    c.section
                    member: "keyAt"
                    text: """
                        /* POSITIONAL reads. `entries` is dense and insertion-ordered, so position is a
                           meaningful coordinate here in a way it is not in a Map, and reaching one
                           costs an array index rather than a probe. They exist because an ordered walk
                           whose body needs the container's owner cannot hold an iterator across it.
                           `keyAt` answers a bare key -- the `idkey` bound makes it a scalar, and 0
                           past the end is the same "no id" every id space already spells -- while
                           `valueAt` answers `get`'s own return, since a value is not always a scalar
                           and not always a copy. */
                        static @@KEY_T@@ z_@@NAME@@_keyAt(const z_@@NAME@@_t* _this, uint64_t _i);
                        static @@KEY_T@@ z_@@NAME@@_keyAt(const z_@@NAME@@_t* _this, uint64_t _i) {
                            if (_i >= (uint64_t)_this->length) return (@@KEY_T@@)0;
                            return _this->entries[_i].key;
                        }
                        """
